    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 041125\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n425 AM MST Fri Sep 4 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 12Z Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Residual tropical moisture will continue to stream into southeastern\n  AZ on Friday, promoting scattered showers and storms across the\n  foothills and higher terrain areas east of Phoenix\n\n- The moisture axis and corresponding rain chances will shift \n  west into SE California and SW Arizona on Saturday and Sunday.\n\n- Temperatures will remain in a near to slightly below normal category\n  through this weekend with highs warming back above normal early\n  next week, resulting in HeatRisk increasing from widespread \n  minor to moderate.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nLatest wv imagery and 500 mb streamline analysis reveals an \nelongated area of high pressure extending from the Southern Plains\ninto the southeastern U.S. while a broad trough encompasses the \nPacific Northwest and much of the West Coast. Our forecast area \nremains centered between both of these features, resulting in \ncontinued dry southwest flow aloft. Meanwhile, Hurricane Marie is \nchurning over the E Pacific, well southwest of the tip of the Baja\nPeninsula. A plume of moisture from this storm continues to \nadvect through N Mexico and into southeastern AZ overnight. \nIsolated showers with a few embedded lightning strikes have been \nforming over the foothills and high terrain NE of Phoenix, and \nthis will likely continue overnight and into early this morning \nwhere PoPs range from 30-50%. By this afternoon, a sharp moisture \ngradient will again be present across the lower deserts as drier \nair associated with the Pacific trough overspreads SE California \nand SW Arizona. Although PWATs in southcentral AZ will remain \nmeager around 1.4- 1.5\", there will be just enough late afternoon \ninstability (MUCAPE ~500 J/kg) for a few pop up showers and storms\nin Central Maricopa and N Pinal Counties. Any shower activity \nwill likely be short-lived and wane quickly after sunset due to \nloss of sfc heating. \n\nHurricane Marie will continue to track slowly northwestward into \nopen waters through this weekend which will allow additional \nmoisture to be transported nwd into SoCal and southern AZ as PWATs \nrise to around 1.6-1.8\" areawide. On Saturday, ridging aloft will \nalso begin to strengthen over the southern Plains and retrograde \nwwd, causing the subtropical jet axis to shift over SE California \nand western AZ. Therefore, we will see an abrupt shift in PoPs from \nE to W with shower activity becoming focused over SE California and \nSW Arizona by Saturday afternoon. Due to increasing moisture and \nextensive cloud cover, instability and lapse rates will be poor and \nthus the threat for any heavy rainfall will remain low. Rainfall \naccumulations across the western half of the forecast area look to \nremain light, around 0.10\" or less through Sunday. The latest WPC \nQPF totals still indicated void in precipitation across southcentral \nAZ including the Phoenix Metro Saturday and Sunday. \n\nDue to the presence of troughing to our west helping to maintain 500 \nmb hghts within a 588-590 dam range, temperatures will remain near \nto slightly below normal through this weekend. Lower desert highs \nwill range from the upper 90s to lower 100s each afternoon with \nSaturday being the \"coolest\" day overall. Morning lows will also \nremain near normal mainly in the upper 70s to middle 80s. The \nassociated height falls from the West Coast trough will also result \nin afternoon/early evening breeziness across portions of southeast \nCA and southwest AZ with peak gusts around 20-30 mph, potentially\nreaching up to 40 mph in the typical wind prone areas of far \nsouthwest Imperial County.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/... \nDeterministic and ensemble guidance continue to differ on the \nmagnitude and positioning of the subtropical ridge early next week\nwhich will dictate how hot temperatures get and how soon rain \nchances will return to our area. Both the EPS and GEFS show the \nridge strengthening over the Southern Plains through the middle of\nthe week which will allow hghts aloft to increase across much of \nthe Desert Southwest. In turn, temperatures will also increase \nfrom near normal Monday to slightly above normal on Tuesday. The \ntemperature forecast becomes much more unclear heading into the \nmiddle of next week with large spreads in the NBM interquartile \nrange. This is likely due to differences in the magnitude of the \nridge with many EPS members showing a stronger ridge with 500 mb \nhghts approaching 596-598 dam while the GEFS shows slightly lower \noverall hghts. For now NBM temperatures show a cooling trend after\nTuesday which is likely due to an increase in moisture as \nsoutheasterly flow aloft returns to the lower deserts. \n\nPWATs are expected to increase back to around 1.5-1.8\" by the middle \nof next week which will bring rain chances back into the picture by \nthe Wednesday-Thursday timeframe when PoPs increase to around 20-40% \nareawide. The GFS is even showing an easterly wave/inverted trough \narriving into southern AZ along with additional tropical Pacific \nmoisture on Wednesday which could boost rain chances significantly,\nbut we will likely have to wait for more run to run consistency \nas these features are always difficult to resolve at longer time \nscales. Either way, most of the GEFS and EPS members are showing \nmeasurable precipitation across portions of the area by the middle\nof next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1125Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nThe likelihood of another slow transition to westerly winds with a\nperiod of gusty south cross runway winds and then the slight\n(10-20%) possibility of TSRA/SHRA this afternoon and early evening\nwill be the main weather issues this TAF period. Current SE'rly\nwinds are expected to slowly transition to W'rly/SW'rly winds this\nafternoon. The slow transition will cause a period of S'rly\ncrosswinds (150v230), and some occasional gusts in the upper teens\nduring this time will also be possible. Once W'rly winds develop\nthis afternoon, occasional gusts into the upper teens will\ncontinue to be possible through the early evening. Chances for\nTSRA/SHRA late this afternoon and evening are too low (10-20%) to\ninclude in this TAF package. Winds are then expected to be W'rly\ninto the overnight hours before developing their usual E'rly\ncomponent during the overnight hours. SCT mid and high clouds will\nbecome BKN at times this afternoon before gradually clearing out\nthis evening and overnight. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nOccasional late afternoon/early evening gusty winds will be the\nmain weather issue through the TAF period under mostly clear\nskies, with FEW high clouds starting to move in at the end of the\nTAF period. SW gusts 20-25kt will be common at KBLH late \nafternoon while KIPL should experience the most pronounced gusts \nduring the evening. Speeds will relax overnight and early morning \nwhere brief periods of nearly calm conditions will exist. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nIsolated showers and storms will be possible in southcentral AZ \nthis morning and again late this afternoon. Rain chances will \nexpand westward through this weekend, becoming focused over the \nwestern districts Saturday and Sunday. The best chance for any \nwetting rainfall will remain over the higher terrain of eastern \nand southeastern AZ. Afternoon minimum humidities will range from \n7-15% across southeast CA and between 20-35% for the the AZ \ndeserts today and will likely increase above 25% areawide this \nweekend. Overnight recoveries will range from poor across the \nwestern deserts to good across the eastern districts tonight and \nincrease to good areawide this weekend. Winds will remain elevated\nout of the south to southwest, with the strongest gusts in \nSoutheast CA and along the Lower Colorado River Valley peaking \nbetween 20-30 mph this afternoon/early evening. Lighter winds, \ngenerally around 15 mph or less will transpire this weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Salerno\nLONG TERM...Salerno\nAVIATION...Berislavich/18 \nFIRE WEATHER...Salerno\n",
